WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.599 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  it was a little past midnight when I got  the call asking me to knock doors for  Hillary Clinton  the caller was my friend Jason who was  working on the campaign in Pennsylvania  he wanted me to take time off of my job  to help with the final push why me I  asked him isn't there someone else more  qualified at the time I was serving as a  policy advisor at the White House and  what I was actually wondering was isn't  there someone else less overqualified I  was working on change at scale if I got  this policy done on self-driving cars we  would potentially be saving over 40,000  lives per year and if I took time off of  work I would knock how many doors I also  didn't know the first thing about  talking to a stranger at their front  door what I knew about was policy the  decision seemed obvious I could add more  value right where I was sorry I said but  Jason didn't give up you want to make a  difference come knock doors I didn't  believe him but I went anyway because a  friend had asked and I am so glad that I  did because for a long time I thought  that the way to create change was  top-down and that meant advising  government leaders at McKinsey or  creating policy at the White House not  knocking on doors in Pennsylvania maybe  some of you have a similar view but I  want to talk to you about why bottom-up  change matters too knocking on doors can  change elections change you and change  our world today so what does it mean to  knock on doors knocking on doors is  about getting into a neighborhood and  going door-to-door to talk to voters it  has two primary purposes force waiting  voters and increasing turnout knocking  on doors might feel old school but it  makes sense  think about how you change someone's  mind its face to face through a  relationship built on trust  knocking on doors relies on both of  those strategies for persuading voters  it's also an effective tool for driving  turnout talking to voters can provide  the nudge that they need to actually get  out there and vote on Election Day  I remember the first time I knocked on  doors I thought this works I remember  the woman in Pittsburgh who told me she  wasn't going to vote because she didn't  know where her polling location was just  the kind of information I had on hand at  another home a man with a golden  retriever stood at the door and said he  wasn't going to vote because Hillary had  it in the bag  I showed him the latest polling numbers  and he promised me he'd vote after that  experience I dug into the research and  found out that two decades of studies  have shown that the surest way to change  an election outcome is to have high  quality door knocking talking to voters  and getting out the vote can make all  the difference especially when margins  are tight take Virginia in 2017 a heated  local race between Democrat Shelley  Simmons and Republican David Yancey  ended in a tie they ended up deciding  the winner by pulling a name out of a  hat that one race resulted in  Republicans maintaining control of the  State House that means one vote could  have changed why health care wages  schools look like for the state and not  every election is won by one vote but  every election cycle there are races  with razor thin margins  knocking on doors is also essential for  national victories in 2016 120 million  votes were cast for the presidential  election but just a hundred and seven  thousand of those votes in three states  determine the outcome a hundred and  seven thousand votes to put that in  perspective if each of us at the  Business School had spoken with just a  hundred and twenty five voters which by  the way you can do in one day we would  have spoken with all of them yes  elections feel big and daunting but  ultimately they're won by relatively  small numbers and those small numbers  have huge impact knocking on doors can  also change you let's face it it can be  intimidating to knock on a stranger's  door but we also know that it's in  moments of discomfort that we grow the  most and you'll grow tremendously both  as a leader and as a citizen today our  world is not set up to interact with  people who are different from us instead  of taking public transportation we take  movers instead of going to the movies we  opt for Netflix in bed and when we want  a cup of sugar  we don't go to our neighbors we go to  instacart  knocking doors gets you out of that  bubble no to conversations I've had at  the door have ever been the same as a  result I've practiced and built a range  of skills I have more empathy after  listening to the stories of strangers I  take more risk after rejection after  rejection at the door and I'm much more  comfortable in difficult conversations  after navigating some hostile attitudes  all of those skills translate in a  really real way to the workplace if  you're an entrepreneur you'll be that  much better at uncovering the needs of  your customers if you're an investor  you'll be more persuasive when it comes  to raising money for me as a policy  maker I'm much better at analyzing the  effects of my policies and programs on a  range of citizens outside of my  immediate circle I'm also a more  informed voter well I might not be  personally struggling with access to  health care Thank You Stanford Cardinal  care after a conversation in Virginia  Beach with a woman whose daughter was  struggling with diabetes I have this  window into how healthcare policy  affects lives and why it matters to vote  for it  so knocking on doors doesn't just change  elections it also changes you here at  the Business School my peers often say I  want to make a difference but let me  focus on making it to the top first and  then I'll do a lot with that money power  and position to do a lot of good but  we're at a critical moment right now on  climate change on health care on the  future of our workforce our democratic  processes are crumbling some things  aren't reversible and so we have to  start changing our world today when I  came to campus back in the fall I was  excited to be surrounded by my peers who  were eager to get out there and make a  difference when the 2018 midterm  election rolled around 100 students  expressed interest in getting involved  but on the day that it came down to  actually knocking doors only two other  students showed up the district's we  were knocking in was josh Harder's in  central valley california he won the  election but it was so close it took  them a week to call it our door knocking  made a difference very few of us are  probably going to knock on doors as  full-time job that's likely not the way  that we aspire to change lives change  organizations and change the world but  there isn't a trade-off here you can  pursue your career of top-down change  while still knocking on doors today they  aren't mutually exclusive in fact  they're mutually beneficial we have an  election coming up in 2020 pick a race  it could be something local it could be  for the next president of the United  States and commit to getting into a  neighborhood even if just once and knock  on some doors I'll see you out there  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

